16
00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:37.213
i): you may use Llama outputs to train or improve another model, but if you distribute that model, its name must start with “Llama”, alongside the license’s other attribution and use terms.

17
00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:43.083
Permissively licensed open models, such as OpenAI’s gpt-oss (Apache 2.

18
00:01:43.363 --> 00:01:53.411
0), put no such restriction in the license itself; still read each model card, since licenses vary by model and some come with a separate usage policy.

19
00:01:53.691 --> 00:01:55.419
Notice the shape of the problem.

20
00:01:55.699 --> 00:02:01.053
The restrictions turn on words like “competing”, which your company’s lawyers interpret, not you.

21
00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:04.533
Enterprise agreements can differ from the public versions.

22
00:02:04.813 --> 00:02:10.616
And terms change, so the version that governs your account is the one in effect when you use the service.

23
00:02:10.896 --> 00:02:25.253
The engineering contribution is to make the question visible: record which model produced each label, under which account, on what date, so that the legal review has facts to work with and you can remove a source’s labels if the answer is no.
